@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/agora/sensing#> .
@prefix water: <http://example.org/agora/water#> .
@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/agora/mqtt#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

<http://example.org/agora/core> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora core" ;
    rdfs:comment """The society kernel: agents, the capabilities they compose, and the versioned world they share. Everything else is a MODULE layered on this — the microcontrollers it runs on, the protocols their parts speak, the parts themselves, the capabilities, and the domain. Code may reference the terms defined here; it may never reference an instance.

What is NOT here any more is everything physical. `packages/part/microcontroller` holds boards, peripherals, pins and wires; a concrete part is its own package beside it. The two subjects were sharing a file and growing at different rates: adding a sensor should not touch what a society IS.""" .

#################  Agents and capabilities (composition, not hierarchy)  #################

ag:Agent a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A self-interested principal with a stake. What an agent can DO is not its class — it is the set of capabilities composed onto it, so two agents of the same kind may run different modules." .

ag:Capability a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "One thing an agent can do, defined by a module: a vocabulary (this ontology), rules (its SHACL shapes), and the code that reads them. Declaring a capability is what activates the module." .

ag:hasCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "has capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "Composed onto the agent in the WORLD graph (public: peers must know what a counterparty can do). The PARAMETERS of a capability are private, in the agent's own beliefs." .

ag:actsFor a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "acts for" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject whose interest this agent advances. Generic; a domain narrows the range (in the water domain, a Plant)." .

#################  Devices — dumb mechanism at the edge  #################

ag:Device a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Device" ;
    rdfs:comment "A physical edge node: mechanism, never policy, and interchangeable with a simulated one." .

#################  Devices that are not there  #################
# A simulated device is an ORDINARY device in every statement the world makes about it: it is
# on a bus, it has topics, it has a sense mode, and an agent is wired to it with sensing:polls like
# any other. The only extra fact is that a process stands in for it.
#
# The marker is on the DEVICE and never on the agent, and that is the whole design. An agent
# that could tell would be a second code path, and a second code path is what a simulation is
# supposed to avoid: world/simulation used to exercise its own implementation, so it could pass
# while the real one was broken.

ag:simulatedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Device ; rdfs:range ag:DeviceModel ;
    rdfs:label "simulated by" ;
    rdfs:comment "No such device exists; a process stands in for it, publishing on the same topics and speaking the same protocol. Read by the generators, which give it a container and a credential, and by nothing an agent runs — an agent must not be able to tell, and the surest way is that nothing it executes ever asks." .

ag:modelMinValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "model minimum" ;
    rdfs:comment "The floor of what this stand-in can report. A moisture fraction happens to run 0..1; a thermometer does not, so the range is stated rather than assumed." .
ag:modelMaxValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "model maximum" .

ag:DeviceModel a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Device model" ;
    rdfs:comment "The physics the stand-in computes between readings. Facts about the thing being modelled rather than opinions about it, which is why they sit on the device and not in anybody's beliefs." .

ag:modelInitialValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model initial value" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it reads before anything has happened to it." .

ag:modelDoseEffect a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model dose effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a dispensed unit moves this modelled value, SIGNED — in units of this value per litre on the dose wire, the litre being the WIRE's unit (a valve's status message speaks ml; that is actuation's physical reality, so it lives in this comment and not in the term's name — the first draft said modelDoseLitres, and the kernel wearing the water domain's word is the exact smell modelLosesPerDay had just been cured of). A pot's conversion arrives through the domain's denomination and never through this; a SOURCE'S effect is not per-instance configuration at all but CONSERVATION — every source loses exactly what was dispensed from it — so the water domain states -1.0 once, on the WaterSource class, and the closure entails it onto every barrel. Read from the SUBJECT by the generation, like the ceiling and the drying." .

ag:modelLosesPerDay a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    # Un-domained since #164: stated on a DeviceModel as an override, or entailed onto a
    # SUBJECT from a domain's own physics term (water:driesPerDay rdfs:subPropertyOf this),
    # so the generator reads one kernel term and the pot keeps the only copy of its fact.
    rdfs:label "model dries per day" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much of the observed property is LOST per simulated day — first-order decay toward the model's floor, for any value that drains: a pot's moisture, a barrel's evaporation, a battery's self-discharge. Was modelDriesPerDay, a kernel term wearing the water domain's word (every-term-in-its-own-house had it listed: only a plant dries); before that ag:modelDryRate, per tick, and the tick made the physics an artifact of attention: the pot dried one step per reading, so a closely-watched plant dried faster than an ignored one — backwards, and visibly so once the cadence machinery started varying how closely anyone watched. Time-based now, scaled by the world's ag:timeScale, so the pot loses what the day costs it however often it is read. Since #164 the domain's water:driesPerDay is a subproperty of this: a pot states its physics once, in its own vocabulary, and the closure entails the kernel's term the generation reads — while a DeviceModel may still state this directly, as an override for values that are not anyone's pot." .

ag:modelDailySwing a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model daily swing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The amplitude of a daily sine around the value — for properties with a diurnal cycle, which soil moisture does not have and air temperature does. One simulated day per period, so under ag:timeScale 24 a bench hour draws a whole day's curve. Absent means flat, which is what the old ever-falling drift pretended not to be." .

ag:modelTickSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "model tick seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wall-clock seconds per tick — the PUBLISH interval of a sensing:PushProcedure device, which keeps its own clock; a sensing:ScheduledProcedure one keeps the interval its agent gives it, exactly as a board does. This used to also pace the physics, which is the artifact ag:modelLosesPerDay records; physics run on the clock now, and the tick is only a rhythm of speech." .

ag:timeScale a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "time scale" ;
    rdfs:comment "Simulated seconds per wall-clock second — 24 makes one bench hour a simulated day. A fact about the WORLD, not any device: every stand-in in a world must age at the same rate or their physics stop composing (a dose sized for a day's drying must land against a day's drying). The agents are deliberately not told: they act in real time against a world that happens to live fast, which changes nothing about how they decide and everything about how much of their behaviour a bench hour shows." .

ag:strayDoseMeanDays a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stray dose mean days" ;
    rdfs:comment "The mean interval, in simulated days, between waterings NOBODY in the society decided — a human with a watering can, rain through a window. Exponentially distributed, per subject that states an ag:rainTopic. Absent means nobody meddles. Stating it gives the world ONE meddler: a container of its own (firmware/simulated-meddler) with a credential of its own, granted WRITE on each rain topic and nothing else — a pot must not water itself, and rain must never ride the valve's status topic, which is the valve's testimony. This is the third-party impulse of #151 made routine: the signal changes without any agent's doing, and the society's only defence is how it samples." .

ag:rainTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "rain topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where water from OUTSIDE the society arrives for this subject — the meddler writes it, the subject's simulated sensor reads it, and nothing else may touch it. On the SUBJECT, because rain falls on a pot and not on an instrument; a simulated world without one for a subject keeps that subject dry except by the market. The payload is the dose report's shape ({\"ml\": N}) because millilitres are millilitres to soil — the indistinguishability is the point, not an economy." .

#################  Where a society executes  #################
ag:ComputeHost a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Device ; rdfs:label "Compute host" ;
    rdfs:comment "A machine that runs agent processes. Named so the world can say where its society actually executes." .

ag:lanHost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:ComputeHost ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "LAN host" ;
    rdfs:comment """How this host is reached by things NOT on it. A co-located agent reaches the broker at mqtt:brokerHost, which is loopback and must be, because host-networked agents have to agree on one name. A board on the wifi cannot use that name, so it needs a way to the machine running the broker. Two routes to one broker, and only the second is a fact about the network rather than about the society.

**A NAME, and that is the whole of why this is not `lanHost` any more.** An address here was a DHCP lease, and the old comment admitted it: "a fact with an expiry date". It expired three times — .126, then .130, then .133 — and the third cost a day, because a board went on dialling .130 and simply fell silent. Nothing in a world can be true about an address a router is free to reassign.

A hostname does not expire. `raspberry.local` is answered by the avahi already running on that host, and ESP32's lwIP resolves `.local` because `CONFIG_LWIP_DNS_SUPPORT_MDNS_QUERIES` is set in the framework this project builds against — so nothing in the firmware changes: `mqtt.setServer` has always taken a name. A router's own domain (`raspberry.speedport.ip` here) works equally well over ordinary DNS and is the fallback if multicast is ever blocked; it is not the default because it couples a world to a brand of router.

An address is still a legal value — some networks have no name service — and stating one is choosing to own the consequence.""" .

ag:runsOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range ag:ComputeHost ; rdfs:label "runs on" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which host executes this agent. Documents why the bus is where it is." .

#################  Naming — stated, never derived  #################

ag:localId a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "local id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The short name a resource is known by outside RDF. Stated here so nothing has to infer it from the shape of a URI. The ONE instance identifier a process is given is an agent's localId; everything else it discovers." .

#################  The store every agent keeps, and what it may cost  #################
#  Not a capability, and the distinction is the one AGENTS.md rule 2 draws: a capability is an
#  ability whose HOW could differ. Reviewing can be done by rule or by asking a model; compacting
#  cannot be done two ways. Keeping your own house is not a choice, so it is kernel — and it has
#  a clock no capability owns, because an agent granted no room to review itself must still
#  compact. See agora/upkeep.py and issue #45.

ag:BeliefBase a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Belief base" ;
    rdfs:comment "The store one agent keeps everything it knows in — the world it was given, what it has sensed, and its own beliefs. A class rather than a graph, because it is the whole store and not one graph inside it: what the agent BELIEVES is the sum of them. Named so that facts true of every agent's store have somewhere to be stated that no capability owns." .

ag:maxBytesPerTriple a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "max bytes per triple" ;
    rdfs:comment "Above this ratio a belief base is mostly write amplification rather than knowledge, and is compacted. Stated here rather than compiled in, for the same reason the cadence bounds are: it is a fact about what this society tolerates, not about how the code happens to be written. It hung off the review capability until that left the kernel — a threshold every agent is held to cannot be owned by a package an agent may not have." .

ag:BeliefBase ag:maxBytesPerTriple 800 .

ag:resignAfterS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "resign after seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long an agent endures a dead bus before it resigns and lets its container bring it back fresh (#53). The fault this bounds is the one that cost two days: a session that ends and never returns, inside a container that looks perfectly healthy, on a bus whose broker never restarted. Ten minutes is far past any reconnect paho would ever achieve — a link that flaps recovers in seconds and never meets this — and far under the hours a quiet failure used to cost. Stated here rather than compiled in, like the compaction ratio above: it is what this society tolerates, not how the code happens to be written." .

ag:Agent ag:resignAfterS 600 .

#################  Identity keys — published, because a verifier cannot read secrets  #########
#
#  The public halves of each agent's keypairs (#144, #145), written by `agora-keygen` into a
#  generated `keys.ttl` beside the world's ratified files. Public by nature and in the world
#  because the world is the one place every member already reads: the host verifying a
#  presentation cannot read the presenter's secrets — that is the isolation — so the roster is
#  how identities are attested. Base64 of the raw 32 bytes, no PEM ceremony.
#
#  TWO keys because the operations differ and conflating them is the classic mistake: Ed25519
#  SIGNS (a presentation under the winner's own hand, #144) and X25519 SEALS (a claim only
#  its winner can open, #145). Absence is the pre-key era and stays legal — a world onboarded
#  before keygen learned agents behaves exactly as it always did.

ag:signingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "signing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "Ed25519 public key, base64 raw. What a presentation is verified against: the durable identity exercising an ephemeral grant, per knowledge/decisions/authn-authz-capabilities.md." .
ag:sealingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "sealing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "X25519 public key, base64 raw. What a claim is sealed to, so the bus carries an envelope only the winner can open — the ACL keeps agents out, the seal keeps the bus out." .

#################  Who did it, and in what capacity  #################
#  A ROLE, not an identity, and the distinction is the whole point. There is no `ag:Sovereign`
#  agent and there must not be: an installation has several users — one authors a world, another
#  operates it with fewer powers — and which of them was sovereign is a fact about ONE ACTIVITY,
#  not a permanent property of a person. PROV-O models exactly this with `prov:qualifiedAssociation`,
#  and expects the domain to name its own roles; naming one here is using PROV-O, not replacing it.
#
#  The role set is open on purpose. `ag:Operator` and whatever else will be needed are absent
#  because nothing distinguishes them yet, and a role no activity cites is speculation.

ag:Sovereign a prov:Role ; rdfs:label "Sovereign" ;
    rdfs:comment "Held by whoever ratified a world: authored its files and stands behind them. A role a user has in one ratification, never a kind of user — the same person may hold it for one world and not another, and an installation's other users hold none. See knowledge/decisions/who-put-the-fact-there.md." .

#################  The world and its versions  #################

ag:World a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ratified world produced by genesis. Found by TYPE, never by a well-known IRI." .
ag:WorldVersion a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World version" ;
    rdfs:comment "One ratified version of the world's topology; the chain is append-only." .
ag:currentVersion a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range ag:WorldVersion ;
    rdfs:label "current version" .
ag:versionNumber a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:WorldVersion ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "version number" .
ag:underWorldVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "under world version" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world-version in force when this fact was recorded (provenance)." .

#################  Graphs — typed, self-describing (not magic strings)  #################
#
#  A graph IRI is an INSTANCE. `ag:WorldGraph` is the term code may name;
#  `…/graph/world` is a particular graph and code may not. The classes below are the terms;
#  the instances at the end of this section are the data — and the split is what lets a reader
#  ask for "public knowledge" without any Python knowing which graphs those happen to be.
#
#  They are declared HERE rather than in each world because they are the same in every world:
#  installation-wide, like the shape of the store itself. Only the per-agent ones vary, and
#  those are derived from the roster (rules.ru) rather than written out.

ag:Graph a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Named graph" .

#################  Three axes over one graph  #################
#
#  A named graph is classified THREE ways, independently, and conflating them is why some
#  graphs never found a natural home: what its content ASSERTS, WHOSE it is, and HOW IT
#  ARRIVED. A species' range is a constraint AND public AND derived; an aim is a desire AND
#  private AND asserted; an obligation is a constraint AND private AND received. Nothing but
#  the first axis is about meaning, and the first axis is the one the vocabulary lacked.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md. The classes below say what a graph IS;
#  which graphs are instances of each is data, asked for and never listed — the arrangement
#  ag:PublicGraph already had, generalised to six modalities and five arrivals.

#  --- modality: what the content asserts ---

ag:Modality a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Modality" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a graph's content asserts about the subjects in it. The same subject, property and unit appear in every modality — fern's moisture is observed, bounded, aimed at, moved by a lever, committed to and recorded — so the modality is what a reader must know to interpret a triple, and the one thing the graph vocabulary could not previously say." .

ag:BeliefGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Belief graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What IS: the world as ratified, what the rules and the closure make of it, and what instruments report. NOT the graph historically called `beliefs`, which holds picks and no beliefs at all — that misnaming is what naming the axis exposed." .

ag:ConstraintGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Constraint graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What MAY be: bounds this agent cannot move and is refused for violating. A species' operating and survival ranges, an instrument's working range, the constitution's ceilings, the sovereign's mandates, and a peer's claim — one kind of thing, differing only in who stated it and whether there is anyone to be wronged." .

ag:DesireGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Desire graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent PURSUES: its aim in a property, its picks about its own conduct, and the goals a claim sourced. Everything here is ordered — which is where character will live — and everything here is the agent's, chosen inside constraints it did not choose." .

ag:MenuGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Menu graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent COULD do: the affordance rows, and what others may demand of it. Derived and never authored — a row is a conclusion whose premises are stored, so an authored row could outlive its premise, which is the one hazard this graph must never reintroduce." .

ag:IntentionGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Intention graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent is DOING: commitments adopted and not yet resolved. Small and live, because what is finished belongs to history." .

ag:HistoryGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "History graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent DID: resolved commitments, discharged obligations, doses delivered, amendments woken into. A bounded ring with the series store behind it — nothing points INTO history, because whatever must outlive the window copies what it needs at commit time, which is how an expectation already carries its own baseline." .

#  --- how it arrived ---

ag:Arrival a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Arrival" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a graph's content got there. Five values and no sixth: everything in this system is said, computed one of two ways, done, or heard." .

ag:Asserted a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "asserted" ;
    rdfs:comment "The sovereign said so. True by ratification, not by argument." .
ag:Derived a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "derived" ;
    rdfs:comment "A package's rule concluded it from given facts. Policy: another society could rule otherwise with the same vocabulary." .
ag:Entailed a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "entailed" ;
    rdfs:comment "The vocabulary's own logic implies it — subclass, subproperty, hasValue, property chains. Not policy: any reasoner over these axioms would agree." .
ag:Recorded a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "recorded" ;
    rdfs:comment "This agent's own act, at runtime. Its word about itself." .
ag:Received a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "received" ;
    rdfs:comment "Another party's word: an instrument's reading, a peer's claim. The only arrival whose trustworthiness is a question — an assertion is the sovereign's by definition and a derivation is checkable arithmetic, but this either carries a signature or does not, which is why the graph classification and the trust story are one story." .

ag:arrivedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Graph ; rdfs:range ag:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "arrived by" .

ag:PublicGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Public graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Knowledge every agent in a world may read: the vocabulary, the topology, and what the rules and the RDFS closure made of them. This is the class `store.query` looks for — the default graph of every query is whatever is an instance of THIS, discovered rather than listed, so adding one is a vocabulary edit and touches no code. Membership is deliberately by declaration and not by exclusion: a graph nobody typed is invisible, which is the safe direction to fail — the other way round, a new private graph nobody remembered to exclude would leak into every query." .

#  The existing classes, now saying their modality too. Nothing moves: a graph that was a
#  WorldGraph is a WorldGraph AND a BeliefGraph, because "topology the sovereign ratified" and
#  "an assertion about what is" are answers to different questions.

ag:WorldGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PublicGraph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "World graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Topology and composed capabilities: who is wired to what, and who can do what. Public, sovereign-authored. Covers what the sovereign ratified AND what the rules and the closure made of it — those are three graphs and one role, because what a graph holds and who put it there are different questions. The second is answered in PROV-O; see agora/provenance.py." .
ag:BeliefsGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph , ag:DesireGraph ; rdfs:label "Beliefs graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's private parameters for the capabilities it composed — and a DESIRE graph, which the name has never said: sort its contents and the aim is a desire about the world while the cadence, patience and ceilings are desires about the agent's own conduct, every one a pick inside a mandate. Nothing in it is a belief; what this agent believes is in the sensed and public graphs. The name is kept for now because graph IRIs are load-bearing in deployed volumes and a rename is a migration; the TYPE is what carries the truth. NOT a public graph: reachable only by naming it, which is what makes a review's write boundary checkable." .
ag:SensedGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Sensed graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What sensors read — a belief graph whose content RECEIVED, since a reading is an instrument's word rather than this agent's. Measurement only; never a judgment. NOT public: an agent's readings are its own." .
ag:OntologyGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PublicGraph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Ontology graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The T-Box: this module and every other, plus what RDFS entails of it." .
ag:ProvenanceGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Provenance graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the other graphs ARE, in PROV-O. NOT public, and the reason is use versus mention: these are statements ABOUT graphs, so merging them into the default graph would make an ordinary `?device a ?class` start returning activities." .

ag:beliefsOf a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:BeliefsGraph ; rdfs:range ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "beliefs of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which agent owns this graph — the write boundary, made explicit." .

##  The instances. Data, not terms — code names the classes above and finds these by asking.
##  Three world graphs and two ontology graphs, because the role is what a graph HOLDS and how
##  a fact got there is a separate question — which each instance now answers with ag:arrivedBy
##  rather than by having its arrival encoded in its name.

<http://example.org/agora/graph/ontology>          a ag:OntologyGraph ; ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/ontology/entailed> a ag:OntologyGraph ; ag:arrivedBy ag:Entailed .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/world>             a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/world/derived>     a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Derived .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/world/entailed>    a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Entailed .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/sensed>            a ag:SensedGraph ;   ag:arrivedBy ag:Received .
<http://example.org/agora/graph/provenance>        a ag:ProvenanceGraph .

##  The bounds a subject's stated ranges put on whoever acts for it: an operating region to
##  hold a property inside, and the survival envelope outside which the subject ends. Derived
##  by the desire package's rule, declared HERE like the derived world graph, because a graph
##  is the kernel's furniture whoever fills it — and a rule targets `ag:ConstraintGraph`
##  directly now, since `graph_of_class` refuses ambiguity loudly rather than guessing.
##
##  It was `graph/desire` and typed a region graph, which was wrong twice: the name followed
##  the capability that computes it, and the content is not anybody's want. An agent holding
##  bounds and no aim pursues NOTHING — the running proof that a bound motivates nobody.
<http://example.org/agora/graph/constraint>        a ag:ConstraintGraph , ag:PublicGraph ;
    ag:arrivedBy ag:Derived .


#################  THE MIND'S STATES  #################
#
#  What a mind CONTAINS — desires, obligations, intentions and the means they name — declared
#  in the kernel because they are the lingua franca: four capability packages already had to
#  name them (market's bidding and hosting, deliberation, actuation), and a term many packages
#  must name is nobody's package-private word. `ag:IntentionGraph` holding `intention:Intention`
#  was a split brain the graph axes exposed.
#
#  WHAT STAYS IN THE PACKAGES is the HOW: `desire:Deducing` and `desire:Consulting` are two ways
#  of arriving at a region, `intention:Keeping` one way of keeping a ledger, and each capability
#  keeps its own parameters (a patience, a met-fraction). That is rule 2 unchanged — a family is
#  where the how could differ — and it is why universality was never the test:
#  telemetry-is-a-mandatory-capability settled that a capability every agent holds is still a
#  capability, and the mirror holds here, that a STATE no agent is obliged to have is still the
#  kernel's word for it. `ag:Device` is kernel and most agents hold none.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md.

#################  What a desire IS  #################

ag:Bounds a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Desire" ;
    rdfs:comment """One property, the region to hold it in, and the envelope outside which the subject does not merely suffer but ends. A desire is per PROPERTY and an agent holds as many as its subject states ranges for — which is the whole of what makes a humidity reading actionable where it was previously a number with nowhere to go.

Not a point. A target is a point and remains one, privately, in the agent's beliefs; this is what that point must answer to.""" .

ag:boundedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:Bounds ;
    rdfs:label "desires" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reads as what this agent is trying to bring about. Derived, never written by hand — a world stating one would be authoring an agent's ends instead of the facts they follow from, and the shapes refuse it." .

#  The region itself is stated in schema.org's terms and not in ours, and the reuse is
#  load-bearing rather than thrifty: an operating range's condition says `schema:minValue` /
#  `schema:maxValue` about a property, and so does this. One vocabulary for one kind of claim
#  means the deduction is MAX and MIN over comparable numbers, with nothing to translate — and
#  a reader who knows how to read a plant's range can already read an agent's.
#
#  `ssn:forProperty` for the same reason. It is what a condition uses to say which property it
#  is a range of, and `sosa:ObservableProperty rdfs:subClassOf ssn:Property` makes it well-typed
#  here too.

ag:toleratedMin a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Bounds ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "tolerated minimum" ;
    rdfs:comment """The floor of the survival envelope for this property, when the world states one. Below it the subject does not merely sit outside where it does well — it ends.

Here rather than left in the plant's own range because it is what makes URGENCY meaningful and unit-free. How bad it is to be 0.05 below the region depends entirely on how much room there is below the region, and that figure is asymmetric in exactly the cases that matter: a Zamioculcas comes back from bone dry and does not come back from a rotted rhizome, so the same distance out reads as far more urgent on the wet side. That falls out of the two numbers rather than out of any code, which is why it is worth carrying them here instead of hard-coding a curve.

Optional, and its absence is not a defect. A world that states no survival range simply has an agent whose urgency is scaled by the width of the region instead — a cruder answer, honestly reached.""" .

ag:toleratedMax a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Bounds ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "tolerated maximum" .

#################  The aim — the pick inside a region  #################
#
#  PRIVATE, and the one part of desire that is. A region is deduced and public; the AIM is the
#  point inside it this agent is currently steering for, and it is the last thing left that an
#  agent decides about its own ends — which is exactly why it is a belief in the agent's own
#  graph, why review may move it, and why nothing may default it. "No preference inside my
#  region" is not a stance this vocabulary can express, deliberately: a consumer that needs a
#  point needs the agent to have picked one, and a missing belief is an error rather than a
#  default everywhere in this project.
#
#  This replaces `water:hasTarget`, which fused two things: the point an agent aims at
#  (desire's) and the input a litre-deficit is priced from (the market's). The domain keeps the
#  pricing — `water:litresPerFraction`, `water:maxValuePerL`, and the statement of which
#  property a bid is denominated in — and asks the aim of whoever provides the desire family.
#
#  NOTE ON MIGRATION: this is a structural move, not a rename, so the vocabulary successor
#  mechanism cannot carry a store across it — `water:hasTarget 0.55` has no single term it
#  became. A volume holding the old term is refused loudly at boot with the term named, which
#  is `agent/vocabulary.py`'s designed behaviour for a deletion: deciding what a dead structure
#  meant is authorship, not computation.

ag:Aim a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Aim" ;
    rdfs:comment "One property and the value this agent is holding it toward: the pick inside the region. Shaped to lie inside the region for that same property, and meaningless — refused — for a property the agent holds no region in." .

ag:aims a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:Aim ;
    rdfs:label "aims" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reads as the point this agent is steering one property toward. In the agent's own beliefs graph, never the world: the region answers to the sovereign's facts, the pick inside it answers to nobody — which is what makes it the agent's. Stated with ssn:forProperty and schema:value, the same two terms a region and a condition already use, so a reader that can read either can read this." .

#################  An obligation: a desire someone else sourced  #################
#
#  BOID's O, and the sovereign's reframe of #218: a claim presented against my hardware does
#  not bypass my wanting — it RAISES a want, with provenance saying whose it is. One kind of
#  motivation, one decision path, and an agent's whole conduct is desires pursued through
#  affordances. See knowledge/decisions/an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced.md.
#
#  The guardrail is the one every desire answers to: an agent may RECEIVE a desire and never
#  AUTHOR one. A deduced desire is derived from ranges the world states; an obligation is
#  derived from a signed claim the society issued. Both externally anchored, so the
#  self-satisfaction loophole stays shut from both directions.
#
#  WHOM I may owe is not stored at all — it is topology, derived like the broker ACL from the
#  wiring that says who may present to me (the market package's honoured.rq walks it). What is
#  stored is what I owe NOW, in a graph of my own, because a debt outlives the process that
#  incurred it: a host used to keep its issued claims in memory and forget them on restart.

ag:Obligation a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Bounds ;
    rdfs:label "Obligation" ;
    rdfs:comment "A desire this agent did not source: what it owes another, raised when the society issued that other a claim against this agent's hardware, discharged when the dose it names is confirmed. A subclass of Desire and not a sibling, because everything that reads desire should read this too — urgency, disclosure, and the deliberation that ranges over wants. What differs is only where it came from, which is what prov says." .

ag:owedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "owed to" ;
    rdfs:comment "The counterparty. Always an agent this world's wiring already connects to me — an obligation to a stranger is not a debt, it is a forgery, refused before it becomes a want." .

ag:forClaim a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "for claim" ;
    rdfs:comment "The jti of the claim that sourced it — single-use there, single-use here, so a discharged obligation cannot be raised twice by a replayed presentation." .

ag:presented a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "presented" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the holder has asked yet. An unpresented claim requires NOTHING of me — the holder is waiting for its own watch to be live — and a presented one requires acting now, which is why urgency here is a step rather than a curve. A deadline-shaped curve belongs to the futures seam, where a claim may be held over time." .

ag:owedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "owed at" .
ag:dischargedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "discharged at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while it stands. The same two-timestamps-and-a-reason shape an intention and a revision already have, because a debt paid and a debt forgotten must never look alike." .


#################  What an intention IS  #################

ag:Intention a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """One commitment: reduce the gap in one property, by one means, adopted at a moment, resolved at another or still standing. `ssn:forProperty` names the property — the same term a region, a condition and an aim already use, so the gap an intention is about is joinable to the desire it serves by the vocabulary everything else speaks.

The lifecycle is two timestamps and an outcome, not a state machine: standing is `adoptedAt` without `resolvedAt`, and how it ended is `outcome` — the same shape review:Revision has, for the same reason. A dropped intention keeps its `becauseOf`, because a commitment abandoned without a reason is indistinguishable from one forgotten.""" .

ag:by a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range ag:Means ;
    rdfs:label "by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of act committed to. A MEANS, not a plan: the how stays with whichever capability executes it." .

ag:Means a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Means" .

ag:Observe a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Observe" ;
    rdfs:comment "Look: obtain a reading where the gap is unmeasured or too stale to act on. The first intention every agent adopts, because at birth there is a desired state and no observations — see the decision record. Satisfied by the reading arriving; dropped when whatever needed it stops waiting." .

ag:Acquire a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Acquire" ;
    rdfs:comment "Obtain what would reduce a gap — here, bid in a market. Adopted when a bid is submitted, satisfied by the claim, and standing in between, which is exactly the state `bidding` used to keep as an untyped dict." .

ag:Apply a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Apply" ;
    rdfs:comment "Spend a held claim against the world. REAL since #132, and earlier than the futures market this was reserved for — because the reason to hold a claim turned out to be OBSERVABILITY before it was temporal strategy: never spend a dose you cannot watch land. Adopted when the claim arrives, standing while the holder waits for its watch to be live (the #135 ack at the fast cadence, or the bounded wait), satisfied when the claim is presented on the market's redeem channel. The expectation on the end opens HERE rather than at the claim, because this is when the dose becomes imminent. The futures seam now merely extends the holding period this gave a mechanism to." .

ag:Actuate a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Actuate" ;
    rdfs:comment "Move a property directly: the lever is mine and the resource is mine, so no negotiation — the ladder's second rung (#190), honest exactly where BOTH chains end at the agent: the lever chain (my actuator, plumbed to my subject) and the resource chain (drawing from a source no market offers as its lot — a pot-local pump on the shared barrel still bids, because the market is about the resource, not the lever). Adopted when the reflex picks the cheaper rung over Acquire, satisfied when the self-dose is commanded; the dose itself is still signed, still confirmed, still watched by an expectation — an unconfirmed self-dose is not a delivered one either, and the REA event stands even though it fulfils no exchange." .

ag:Offer a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Offer" ;
    rdfs:comment "The host's move: open a round on a venue it convenes — the sell half of the dealer's acquire-then-offer, named so a PLAN can say its second step in the same vocabulary the keeper records the first in. ADOPTED since #206, by hosting, exactly once per owed round: a LOW nobody can serve defers the round and the deferral is a commitment — held only in module memory it was a promise a restart forgot and no ask could see. Adopted when the vessel is dry, satisfied when the refill lands and the round opens, recovered from the ledger at the next stock reading when the process that owed it has died. Deciding is still nobody's here: physics defers the round, the keeper only remembers it is owed — the whether-to-sell (the strategic-supplier seam) remains untouched." .

ag:adoptedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "adopted at" .
ag:resolvedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "resolved at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while the intention stands. Its absence IS the standing state — no flag to keep in step." .
ag:outcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "How it ended: satisfied or dropped. A string rather than a class per outcome, exactly as review:outcome is." .
ag:becauseOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it was adopted, and — appended on resolution — why it ended. Prose for the operator reading a ledger, never parsed." .

#################  The expectation — the END, judged apart from the means  #################
#
#  `outcome` above says whether the MEANS succeeded: the claim arrived, the look came back.
#  It says nothing about the END — the commitment was to reduce a gap, and an agent whose water
#  never reaches the pot buys, records "satisfied", and buys again forever: transaction
#  confirmed, outcome never audited. Confidently dumb, structurally. Issue #131.
#
#  So an intention whose act should MOVE something carries an expectation: the value it left
#  from (the baseline, copied here at adoption because the sensed graph keeps only the current
#  witness — the ledger is what remembers), which way it should move (the same direction the
#  domain states on its valuation, #127), and by when. The verdict lands beside the outcome as
#  a SEPARATE fact: `endMet`. Satisfied-and-unmet is the false-knowledge signature — the graph
#  said this lever moves this property, and the world kept disagreeing.

ag:expectsValueTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ;
    rdfs:label "expects value to" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way the property should move if the act did what the graph claims — a market:Direction individual, copied from the lever's own statement at expectation time so the row is judgeable without a join to a vocabulary that may since have been amended." .
ag:baselineValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "baseline value" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the property stood when the expectation was adopted. Copied into the row, deliberately: the sensed graph upserts — one current observation per property — so the BEFORE of any before/after survives nowhere but here. The ledger is the memory of commitments, and this is the commitment's opening position." .
ag:baselineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "baseline at" .
ag:deadlineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "deadline at" ;
    rdfs:comment "By when the movement should have shown. The patience serves as the horizon until something derives a better one from the dose and the physics — a recorded seam of #131, not an oversight." .
ag:expectsDelta a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "expects delta" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far the act should move the property, when the actor can say — a dose of known litres through the domain's stated conversion. What the met-verdict measures its margin against (#165): a movement is the world answering only when it is commensurate with the act, and a breath of instrument grain past the baseline is not. Optional, because not every act can size its own effect; absent, the verdict falls back to exact crossing, which is the pre-noise behaviour and stays legal." .


ag:endMet a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "end met" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the world answered as the graph promised: a reading past the baseline in the expected direction before the deadline — and, where the row states an expectsDelta, past it by at least metFraction of that delta (#165) — or the deadline passed without one (false). Absent while the watch is still on. A row satisfied-and-unmet is one false-knowledge datum; enough of them for one (means, property) pair and the affordance is SUSPECT — flagged, never auto-retracted, because deleting knowledge would be reaching down a level." .
ag:endVerifiedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "end verified at" .

